Matt's generic version of `groupBy`:

```ts
const groupBy = <
  TObj extends Record<string, unknown>,
  TKey extends keyof TObj
>(
  arr: TObj[],
  key: TKey
) => {
  const result = {} as Record<
    TObj[TKey] & PropertyKey,
    TObj[]
  >;
  arr.forEach((item) => {
    const resultKey = item[key] as TObj[TKey] &
      PropertyKey;
    if (result[resultKey]) {
      result[resultKey].push(item);
    } else {
      result[resultKey] = [item];
    }
  });
  return result;
};
```

- `TObj` captures the shape of the array's items. `TKey extends keyof TObj` restricts `key` to a real property of those items.
- The return type is `Record<TObj[TKey] & PropertyKey, TObj[]>`, so `groupBy(array, "age")` gives items typed as `{ name: string; age: number }`.
- Matt admits the code is "extremely complex," but says it "perfectly describes the behaviour of the function."
- Payoff for consumers: `result[20].forEach((item) => console.log(item.name, item.age))` has "no errors, no validation needed." Consumers never have to think about `any` or `unknown`.
- Matt's pitch: Total TypeScript Core Volume has a workshop module on generics aimed at helping developers avoid suboptimal code that brings uncertainty into an app.

Matt extends the same idea from state to props: when a component renders in two slightly different ways, use a discriminated union on a `variant` field.

```ts
type ModalProps =
  | {
      variant: "with-description-and-button";
      buttonText: string;
      description: string;
      title: string;
    }
  | {
      variant: "base";
      title: string;
    };
```

- `buttonText` and `description` are required only when `variant` is `"with-description-and-button"`.
- The `base` variant takes only `title`, so callers cannot pass half a configuration.
- His summary: thinking in discriminated states replaces a "big optional bag of data" with a clear understanding of the connections between data and UI.

Functions and classes can declare type parameters too. When they do, they are generic functions and generic classes and can receive type arguments.

```ts
const myFunc = <T>() => {
  // implementation...
};
myFunc<string>();

class MyClass<T> {
  // implementation...
}
new MyClass<string>();
```

- The most popular use is giving type information to third-party libraries.
- `const [message, setMessage] = useState();` gives `message` the type `undefined`, because `useState` has nothing to go on.
- Passing a type argument fixes it: `useState<string>()` gives `message` the type `string | undefined`.

Matt treats a generic type as a "type function" that takes a type argument and returns a new type.

- The problem: `GetUserData`, `GetPostsData` and `GetCommentsData` each repeat the same shape, `data: {...}` plus `error?: ErrorShape`, where `ErrorShape` is `{ message: string; code: number }`.
- The OOP-style fix is a base interface (`DataBaseInterface { error?: ErrorShape }`) that each interface extends. It works but is verbose.
- The more concise fix is a generic type:

```ts
type DataShape<TData> = {
  data: TData;
  error?: ErrorShape;
};

type GetUserData = DataShape<{ id: string; name: string }>;
type GetPostsData = DataShape<{ id: string; title: string }>;
```

- The angle brackets in `<TData>` add a type argument. The name is arbitrary, "just like an argument to a function".
- Generic types can take multiple type arguments, defaults for type arguments, and constraints that limit which types can be passed.
- He flags this syntax as important because it comes up again in the function patterns.

Functions can accept type arguments the same way types do. Matt's example:

```ts
const createSet = <T>() => {
  return new Set<T>();
};

const stringSet = createSet<string>();
// Set<string>
const numberSet = createSet<number>();
// Set<number>
```

- `<T>` goes before the parentheses in the declaration, and is passed on to `Set<T>`, which itself accepts a type argument.
- Calling `createSet<string>()` gives a Set that only accepts strings, so `stringSet.add(123)` errors with "Argument of type 'number' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string'."
- This is the second meaning of "generics": manually passing types to functions.
- The React equivalent is `useState<number>(0)`, which gives `index: number`.
- At this stage `createSet` takes only type arguments, no runtime arguments.

Matt builds on `createSet` by adding a runtime argument typed as `T`:

```ts
export const createSet = <T>(initial: T) => {
  return new Set<T>([initial]);
};
```

- Manually passing a type still works, and is checked against the argument: `createSet<number>("pocock")` errors because a string isn't assignable to `number`.
- The key point: "TypeScript can infer the type of `T` from `initial`." The type argument is inferred from the runtime argument.
- So `createSet("matt")` gives `Set<string>` and `createSet(123)` gives `Set<number>`, with no angle brackets needed.
- Hovering over the call shows `<string>` being inferred, as in `createSet: <string>(initial: string) => Set<string>`.
- This explains the React behavior that confuses people: `useState(0)` infers `number` without `useState<number>(0)`. The hover tooltip shows `useState<number>(initialState: number | (() => number))`.
- He introduces it as "WHAT IS THIS SORCERY" and then explains it as ordinary inference from arguments.

Matt's `objKeys` example combines inference with extra generic features:

```ts
const objKeys = <T extends object>(obj: T) => {
  return Object.keys(obj) as Array<keyof T>;
};

const keys = objKeys({ a: 1, b: 2 });
// ("a" | "b")[]
```

- `T extends object` constrains `T` so it can be passed to `Object.keys`, which only accepts objects.
- The return type is forced to `Array<keyof T>`, because `Object.keys` normally returns `string[]`.
- Because `T` is inferred from the argument, the caller gets the precise union of literal keys without writing any type arguments.

- Matt calls vitest "extremely powerful and popular" and notes it is already widely used for runtime tests, so it makes sense to use it for types too.
- Vitest ships APIs for testing types, including `assertType` and `expectTypeOf`.
- Example from the essay:

```ts
import { assertType, expectTypeOf } from "vitest";
import { mount } from "./mount";

test("my types work properly", () => {
  expectTypeOf(mount).toBeFunction();
  expectTypeOf(mount).parameter(0).toMatchTypeOf<{ name: string }>();
});
```

- Main benefit: it is built into the test runner, so type tests sit next to runtime tests (colocation).

Matt's approach when not using vitest is to use type helpers `Expect` and `Equal` to assert exactly what a type narrows to:

```ts
type Expect<T extends true> = T;
type Equal<X, Y> = (<T>() => T extends X ? 1 : 2) extends <
  T
>() => T extends Y ? 1 : 2
  ? true
  : false;

const identityFunc = <T>(arg: T) => arg;

it("Should return whatever you pass in", () => {
  const test1 = identityFunc("hello");

  type test = Expect<Equal<typeof test1, "hello">>;
});
```

- A failing type test shows up as a red line in the IDE.
- In CI or as part of the test suite, run `tsc` over the whole suite and check whether any type tests fail.
- `Expect<T extends true>` fails to compile unless `Equal` resolves to `true`, which is what makes the failure visible.

- Matt uses `// @ts-expect-error` to check that something does not work. The comment looks at the next line and fails if that line has no error.
- Use it to assert that a certain input is rejected:

```ts
const myFunc = <T extends string>(arg: T) => {};

it("Should not accept a number", () => {
  // @ts-expect-error
  myFunc(123);
});
```

- He admits the limitation: it is "not exactly perfect" because it is not granular. You cannot check that a specific error is thrown, only that an error was thrown.

Matt says he has been through dozens of open-source application codebases, and every one with a large test suite had this problem. His fix is a library, `shoehorn` (`@total-typescript/shoehorn`, on npm and GitHub at `total-typescript/shoehorn`).

- It gives tests a sanctioned way to pass the wrong or partial type, replacing ad hoc `as any` and `as unknown as X`.
- Its `fromPartial` helper takes a partial object and returns something accepted where the full type is expected.

Example from the article:

```ts
import { fromPartial } from "@total-typescript/shoehorn";

it("Should get the user", () => {
  handler(
    fromPartial({
      body: {
        id: "123",
      },
    }),
    fromPartial({})
  );
});
```

- The request mock supplies only `body: { id: "123" }`.
- The response mock is an empty `fromPartial({})`.
- No explicit `Request` or `Response` import or cast is needed in the test.

Matt argues that understanding how the global type scope works in TypeScript is critical. His reasoning: "Love them or hate them, globals are a part of the way we use JavaScript." If globals are used in JavaScript, they need to be described in TypeScript.

- `declare global` types functions and variables directly in the global scope:
  ```ts
  declare global {
    function myFunc(): boolean;
    var myVar: number;
  }
  console.log(myVar);
  console.log(myFunc());
  ```
- The same techniques add types to `process.env`, type `Window`, and create your own global types.

Type predicates let a function declare that its boolean return value describes one of its arguments.

- Example: filtering `undefined` out of an array.
  ```ts
  const values = ["a", "b", undefined, "c", undefined];
  const filteredValues = values.filter(
    (value): value is string => Boolean(value),
  );
  // filteredValues is now string[]
  ```
- Without the `value is string` predicate, `filter` does not narrow the element type. With it, the result is `string[]`.
- Matt's framing: both type predicates and assertion functions "improve TypeScript's ability to narrow your code".

Assertion functions work similarly to type predicates, but throw an error inside the function to "assert" that the value passed corresponds to a certain type.

- Example:
  ```ts
  function assertUserIsAdmin(
    user: NormalUser | AdminUser,
  ): asserts user is AdminUser {
    if (user.role !== "admin") {
      throw new Error("Not an admin user");
    }
  }
  ```
- After calling the function, TypeScript treats `user` as `AdminUser` for the rest of the scope.
- Use a type predicate when the function returns a boolean you branch on. Use an assertion function when you want to throw on failure and keep going with a narrowed type.

Matt's fourth pattern is classes, which he acknowledges is surprising ("Classes?! Yes."). The humble ES6 class, in TypeScript's hands, is an amazing tool for the builder pattern.

- The builder pattern is at the core of tRPC, one of TypeScript's most popular libraries.
- Combined with assertion functions, a class can type its own shape "from inside the class itself":
  ```ts
  export class SDK {
    loggedInUser?: User;

    constructor(loggedInUser?: User) {
      this.loggedInUser = loggedInUser;
    }

    assertIsLoggedIn(): asserts this is this & { loggedInUser: User } {
      if (!this.loggedInUser) {
        throw new Error("Not logged in");
      }
    }
  }
  ```
- After `sdk.assertIsLoggedIn()`, `sdk.loggedInUser` is narrowed from `User | undefined` to `User`, using `asserts this is this & { ... }`.
